AWS DNS + GoDaddy + Route 53 + ACM – Complete Flow

STEP 1 – Register the domain in GoDaddy

1. Go to GoDaddy.
2. Search and purchase your domain.
   Example:
   myapp.com

GoDaddy is the DOMAIN REGISTRAR.

Domain:
myapp.com


STEP 2 – Create Hosted Zone in AWS Route 53

1. AWS Console → Route 53.
2. Go to Hosted Zones.
3. Click Create Hosted Zone.
4. Enter:
   Domain name: myapp.com
5. Select:
   Public hosted zone
6. Click Create Hosted Zone.

Route 53 automatically creates:

SOA record
NS record


STEP 3 – Copy Route 53 Nameservers

Inside the Route 53 Hosted Zone, find the NS record.

You will see something similar to:

ns-123.awsdns-10.com
ns-456.awsdns-20.net
ns-789.awsdns-30.org
ns-012.awsdns-40.co.uk

Copy these 4 nameservers.


STEP 4 – Update Nameservers in GoDaddy

1. Go to GoDaddy.
2. My Products → Domains.
3. Select your domain.
4. Go to DNS / Nameservers.
5. Choose:
   I'll use my own nameservers
6. Replace GoDaddy nameservers with the 4 Route 53 nameservers.

Example:

GoDaddy
   ↓
Nameservers
   ↓
ns-123.awsdns-10.com
ns-456.awsdns-20.net
ns-789.awsdns-30.org
ns-012.awsdns-40.co.uk

Save the changes.

Now:

GoDaddy = Domain Registrar
Route 53 = DNS Provider


STEP 5 – Create ACM Certificate

1. AWS Console → Certificate Manager (ACM).
2. Make sure you select the AWS Region where the certificate will be used.
   Example:
   ap-south-1
3. Click Request.
4. Select Public Certificate.
5. Enter your domain:

myapp.com

Optionally:

*.myapp.com

6. Select:
   DNS validation
7. Click Request.


STEP 6 – ACM Gives You a CNAME

ACM will provide something similar to:

Name:
_abc123.myapp.com

Type:
CNAME

Value:
_xyz456.acm-validations.aws.


STEP 7 – Add ACM CNAME to Route 53

Go to:

Route 53
→ Hosted Zones
→ myapp.com
→ Create Record

Enter the CNAME provided by ACM.

Record type:
CNAME

Record name:
_abc123

Value:
_xyz456.acm-validations.aws.

Save the record.


STEP 8 – ACM Validates the Domain

ACM checks the CNAME record.

Flow:

ACM
 ↓
Checks CNAME
 ↓
Route 53
 ↓
DNS validation successful
 ↓
ACM Certificate
 ↓
ISSUED


STEP 9 – Create Application DNS Record

Suppose your application is running behind an AWS Application Load Balancer.

You want:

https://app.myapp.com

Then create a Route 53 record:

Record name:
app

Record type:
A

Alias:
ON

Route traffic to:
Application Load Balancer


Final flow:

User
 ↓
https://app.myapp.com
 ↓
Route 53
 ↓
A / Alias Record
 ↓
AWS Load Balancer
 ↓
EKS Ingress
 ↓
Kubernetes Service
 ↓
Pod
 ↓
Application


IMPORTANT – Remember These Rules

1. GoDaddy
   = Domain Registrar

2. Route 53
   = DNS Provider

3. Route 53 Hosted Zone
   = Container for DNS records

4. NS record
   = Tells the internet which DNS servers manage the domain

5. SOA record
   = DNS authority information
   Usually automatically created by Route 53

6. ACM CNAME
   = Used only for certificate/domain validation

7. A record / Alias
   = Used to send application traffic to your application/load balancer

8. ACM does NOT create your Hosted Zone.

9. Route 53 creates the Hosted Zone.

10. ACM provides the CNAME value for DNS validation.

Golden Flow:

GoDaddy
   ↓
Register Domain
   ↓
Route 53
   ↓
Create Public Hosted Zone
   ↓
Get NS Records
   ↓
Update GoDaddy Nameservers
   ↓
Route 53 becomes DNS Provider
   ↓
ACM
   ↓
Request SSL Certificate
   ↓
DNS Validation
   ↓
ACM gives CNAME
   ↓
Add CNAME to Route 53
   ↓
Certificate ISSUED
   ↓
Create A/AAAA/ALIAS record
   ↓
Domain points to Load Balancer/Application
